@1 = ~Can I help you...? Wait. Have we met before?~
@2 = ~Bassilus? Is that you?~
@3 = ~You're that delusional madman who murdered innocent people near Beregost. I thought I killed you.~
@4 = ~I don't think we've met. No.~
@5 = ~Welcome back. I've been keeping busy tending this sanctuary. Every day is another chance to make amends.~
@6 = ~How are you finding life in Helm's temple?~
@7 = ~Now I remember. You put an end to my madness near Beregost. I could never repay you for all you did for me that day.~
@8 = ~Cyric would eventually have led me to my doom. I craved nothing but peace of mind. My family's death shattered me, and I lost touch with reality.~
@9 = ~My life was never an honourable one. I lied, schemed and plotted to ruin anyone who stood in my way. I'm tired of it. I've abandoned Cyric and am trying to make a better life for myself.~
@10 = ~What would you like to know?~
@11 = ~What happened to you after Beregost?~
@12 = ~Leaving Cyric for Helm, huh?~
@13 = ~I may not have killed you properly back then, but I will now if you give me reason. Keep your distance.~
@14 = ~My mistake. My name is Bassilus. Bassilus Fennerys. It's a pleasure to meet you. I recognise you, though we've never met. You helped put an end to the crisis up north. Word of your deeds reached me even while I was in hiding.~
@15 = ~It pains me to admit it, but I was once a murderer. I lost my sanity when I became convinced that giants had destroyed my home. I fled in terror, and Cyric's influence overwhelmed me. I scarcely knew what I was doing. Many innocent people paid with their lives.~
@16 = ~After my death, Kelddath, the high priest of Lathander in Beregost, found me and brought me back. Strangely, death itself seemed to break Cyric's hold on my mind. Kelddath sheltered me from those who would have burned me at the stake.~
@17 = ~Kelddath, the high priest of Lathander in Beregost, found me and resurrected me. Oddly enough, your killing me seems to have broken Cyric's hold on my mind. Kelddath sheltered me from those who would gladly have burned me at the stake.~
@18 = ~He fed and clothed me, and kept me away from the townsfolk's anger. I couldn't stay at his side forever; someone would have recognised me. With his help, I joined a caravan and made my way to Athkatla. Homeless, jobless and despised, I found a little purpose here at Helm's temple, helping the priest maintain it.~
@19 = ~Nobody here knows my past. To them, I'm simply a cleaner. I look after the sanctuary, and the priests allow me to stay.~
@20 = ~I lost my priesthood and all my powers. At the temple of Lathander, I spent months fasting, praying and undergoing rigorous tests of faith to prove my mind was free from Cyric's lies. With Kelddath's help, I was able to avoid the punishment Cyric would have inflicted upon an apostate.~
@21 = ~I'm not the man I once was. I know that words cannot undo the harm I caused, but I mean to spend what remains of my life making amends.~
@22 = ~Thank you for hearing me out.~
@23 = ~I'm glad you're better now, Bassilus, and staying out of trouble. You're doing better than I dared hope.~
@24 = ~If that's the case, I'm glad you're better now, and staying out of trouble.~
@25 = ~You've come a long way. I hope your life finally settles down and gives you some peace.~
@26 = ~You've come a long way. I hope your life finally settles down and gives you some peace.~
@27 = ~I do not condone murder, nor do I wish to remain in the company of a murderer. I'll take my leave.~
@28 = ~I'm glad we had this chance to speak. It's not every day I can unburden myself to someone who remembers what I was. I wish you and your friends good fortune. Oh, one moment—please, take this sword. I have no more use for such a thing.~
@29 = ~I'm glad we had this chance to speak. It's not every day I can unburden myself to someone who understands the dangers of the road. I wish you and your friends good fortune.~
@30 = ~Thank you, my friends. I'm glad we've had this chance to speak. I wish you all good fortune on your travels. One moment, though—please take this sword. I have no use for a weapon like this any longer.~
@31 = ~Thank you, my friends. I'm glad we've had this chance to speak. I wish you all good fortune on your travels.~
@32 = ~Your words hurt me, but I understand. Many people will never forgive what I've done. I should get back to cleaning. Take care of yourselves.~
@33 = ~Oh, I'm no priest of Helm. I'm merely a cleaner here. I owe my life to the priests of Lathander and Helm, but for now I've put aside worship altogether. I need time to think and decide which god, if any, I can truly serve. Perhaps one day I'll seek priesthood in the Temple District. But not yet.~
@34 = ~Is there anything else you wanted to ask?~
@35 = ~What happened to you after Beregost?~
@36 = ~I'll leave you to your work.~
@37 = ~I may not have killed you properly back then, but I will now if you give me reason. Keep your distance.~
@38 = ~I... understand. Your words hurt, but you've every reason to mistrust me. Many will never accept that I am trying to atone. Take care of yourselves, all right?~
@39 = ~I clean, carry supplies and help keep this place orderly. It isn't much, but for once I can rest without fearing what I might do tomorrow.~
@40 = ~By Helm's watchful gaze... where did you find this? I know this symbol. It belonged to Hannibal, before he took to piracy and bloodshed. He once served here as a priest of Helm, but he strayed from his vows and chose a darker path. I had feared the worst; now I see he kept the symbol even after abandoning his faith.~
@41 = ~We found it on Hannibal's body. He's dead, and his followers will trouble the River District no longer. Take this as proof.~
@42 = ~I would rather keep it for now.~
@43 = ~Then Helm has seen justice done. Thank you for bringing me proof of Hannibal's death. Keep the peace you have won, and accept this blessing for the dangers you faced. Each of you has earned my gratitude.~
@44 = ~Welcome to the Watchful Shield. Are you an adventurer in need of healing, or a soul seeking forgiveness and atonement for past misdeeds?~
@45 = ~I'd like to use the services of the temple.~
@46 = ~What do you think of Bassilus?~
@47 = ~I have to go.~
@48 = ~It isn't a Helmite's duty to condemn a man forever for his past, but to help set a direction for his future. Bassilus fell victim to a cruel fate. He has since sought help and worked hard to change his ways. I offered him simple employment so that he might sustain himself until he is ready to join a temple and serve a god of his choosing. His repentance is sincere. As a priest of Helm, I mean to help him remain on that path.~
@49 = ~Now, is there anything I can help you with?~
@50 = ~I'd like to use the services of the temple.~
@51 = ~I have to go.~
@52 = ~I see that I have quite a history to explain.~
@53 = ~I would advise caution. Bassilus came from Zhentil Keep, a place steeped in corruption. A clear mind does not erase his past misdeeds. I hope I'm wrong, but we shouldn't assume his innocence so readily.~
@54 = ~Boo does not smell evil in this man! We should still be careful, but perhaps the blow that felled him struck the madness from his head as well. Boo would give him a chance!~
@55 = ~I don't know Bassilus as you do. I gather he was hardly an honourable man when you last met, but look at him. There is pain in his eyes, and I can't believe it is feigned.~
@56 = ~All I ever craved was a little peace of mind. And Cyric would surely have led me to my doom.~
@57 = ~The priests of Lathander gave me a chance to make amends, but freedom from Cyric did not come easily.~
@58 = ~Escaping Cyric is not so simple as renouncing his service. His madness can overwhelm those who turn from him, or the god himself may send assassins to punish their desertion.~
@59 = ~That is why my penance was long and carefully supervised.~
@60 = ~I cannot ask you to forgive me.~
@61 = ~I'm not certain we have treated this poor man fairly. He does seem to be trying to change.~
@62 = ~Your caution is understandable.~
@63 = ~I remember how mad he was near Beregost. I can't excuse what he did, but an evil god twisted his mind. After what Irenicus did to me, I know something about suffering and being made to feel helpless. I can see he's sorry, even if I still feel uneasy around him.~
@64 = ~Cyric is petty, vindictive and profoundly paranoid. He does not forgive apostates; his priests hunt them mercilessly. Giving a warning is not my usual habit, but I would watch my back if I were you.~
